					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-13891312</guid>
				<title>SCP-5233</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-13891312/scp-5233</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Tue, 01 Dec 2020 05:28:46 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Zyn</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1404533</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>Big big big thanks to my lovely reviewers in chat: Pratten, Savan, Calibri_Bold, Mars, chaucer345, and Karakatt for all your wonderful suggestions to better develop the backstory here!</p> <p>Image source is here: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_earwig_(Forficula_auricularia)_on_thumb.jpg">https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_earwig_(Forficula_auricularia)_on_thumb.jpg</a></p> <p>Notes:<br /> - All of Wiggy’s interview excerpt dialogue consists of words found in the Wikipedia article for Earwig: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earwig">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earwig</a><br /> - Why earwigs? 1. When I was in elementary school, my friends and I found a very large pincher bug on the playground and admired it. Some other kid picked it up and tore off its pincers to keep as a souvenir. That image has stuck with me for over a decade. I can’t smush earwigs anymore. Also last month I found one in a box at work the other day and I let him go outside and it reminded me of that incident. 2. Quoth Wikipedia, “Earwigs are among the few non-social insect species that show maternal care.” Pinchy pinch moms are good moms. There’s a lot of neat facts about earwigs.<br /> - Why Arizona? During my background research, I found this: <a href="https://cals.arizona.edu/yavapai/anr/hort/byg/archive/earwig.html">https://cals.arizona.edu/yavapai/anr/hort/byg/archive/earwig.html</a> so I just went with Arizona.</p> <p>Best wishes to everyone in this crazy year. To everyone who has the opportunity to nurture a life, no matter how small, and those who teach others to do the same, you are the hope of the world.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
